libmath: the floating point of Inferno
======================================

The interface is one header, include/inferno/fp.h.  The Dis VM (libinterp,
emu), limbo and the libraries use nothing else of floating point: no
SoftFloat call, no float64_t field, no host double (except the transport of
the bits through the varargs of printf, inferno_fp_from_host/to_host).

What this directory provides
----------------------------
  fp/softfpu.c	the SoftFPU (control and status words of the running process)
		and the collection of the exception flags
  fp/fpbits.c	bits, halves and host conversions of the numbers
  fdlibm/	the elementary functions (fd_*), written on float64_t
  blas.c gemm.c	inferno_fp_dot, _iamax, _norm1, _norm2, _gemm
  fdim.c	fd_fdim, fd_fmax, fd_fmin
  dtoa.c	fd_strtod, fd_dtoa (integer arithmetic only)
  g_fmt.c gfltconv.c	fd_g_fmt, inferno_fp_fmtconv (the verbs e f g)
  softfloat/	Berkeley SoftFloat 3 (see README.inferno)
  test/		fptest, oracle (fdlibm), dtoatest; and emu/test/math.b for the VM

Replacing it
------------
Another implementation (for example one that uses the arithmetic of the
processor, or SIMD, to be faster) only has to provide, with the same
behaviour, what fp.h declares:

  - the types float64_t and float32_t, and the macros of fp.h that name the
    arithmetic (inferno_fp_add, ..., inferno_fp_from_i32, INFERNO_FP_CONST,
    inferno_fp_pending, inferno_fp_discard...); they may be redefined;
  - the functions inferno_fp_init/save/restore/control/status/get_control/
    get_status/flags/round_nearest/round_restore: the SoftFPU keeps the
    control and status words per process; the arithmetic only raises flags,
    and inferno_fp_flags moves them to the status word and says which ones
    trap; the VM raises the error;
  - the elementary functions fd_*, which round to nearest and only raise flags;
  - the conversions of numbers to text and back (fd_strtod, fd_dtoa, fd_g_fmt,
    inferno_fp_fmtconv): same results, bit for bit.

The behaviour is defined by IEEE 754 binary64 (and binary32 for the
conversions), with the rounding modes of the control word.  It is checked by
emu/test/math.b (run it with `mk test` at the root), libmath/test/oracle
(against the reference copy of fdlibm), fptest and dtoatest.
